  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["C1"],
      "pins": ["C1.1", "C1.2"],
      "nets": ["Display_Power_Switch"],
      "observed": "On the EPD_Driver sheet, the input-decoupling capacitor C1 (connected between the Display_Power_Switch / boost-input node and GND, in parallel with C2 and feeding L1) is drawn with the value 0.1 µF / 16 V.",
      "expected": "The specification's component-matching table states that schematic C1 belongs to the 1 µF / 25 V group (\"C2 default: 1 µF/25 V | C1/C5/C7/C8/C9/C10 = 1 µF/25 V\"), i.e. C1 should be 1 µF / 25 V, matching the Pervasive Displays reference circuit.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "C1  0.1uF/16V  (EPD_Driver.kicad_sch, sheet /EPD_Driver/)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "C2 default: 1 µF/25 V | C1/C5/C7/C8/C9/C10 = 1 µF/25 V | Matches default"}
      ],
      "impact": "C1 is the input bypass capacitor for the EPD boost converter (the pulsed-current load of L1/Q1). Replacing the specified 1 µF with 0.1 µF reduces input filtering by 10×, which can cause rail droop / instability on the Display_Power_Switch node during display refresh and increased EMI; the 16 V rating is also below the specified 25 V (though the rail is 3.3 V, so the rating is a secondary deviation).",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build matches the specification almost everywhere (parts, values, power topology, EPD driver, keyboard, barrel-jack battery-disconnect, Q2 load switch), but the EPD boost-converter input capacitor C1 is drawn as 0.1 µF / 16 V instead of the specified 1 µF / 25 V, a deviation in value (and voltage rating) from the intended reference design."
}

